(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','TOW ZONE — No parking between 2:00 a.m. and 5:00 a.m. for street sweeping. Vehicles left during these hours will be towed at the owner''s expense.','NONE','["Parking is free all night","Sweeping runs at noon only","A car parked at 3:00 a.m. may be towed","The owner pays only a small fine"]','C',1,'Because the tow window covers 2:00–5:00 a.m., a car left at 3:00 a.m. can be towed, which is option C.',16),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the timetable and answer the question.','EXPRESS 40 — Departs the terminal at 6:15, 7:00, and then every 30 minutes until 9:00 p.m. The trip skips the riverfront stop after 7:00 p.m.','NONE','["The last departure is at 9:00 p.m.","Buses run only in the morning","Every trip stops at the riverfront","The gap between buses is one hour"]','A',1,'Departures run up to 9:00 p.m., so the final listed bus leaves at 9:00 p.m.',17),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the membership form and answer the question.','MEMBERSHIP — A student rate needs a valid ID renewed each term. The card is personal; lending it to anyone suspends both accounts for one month.','NONE','["The student rate needs no ID","Sharing the card can suspend two accounts","Lending the card is allowed once","A suspension lasts one week"]','B',1,'The form warns that lending a personal card suspends both accounts, matching option B.',18),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the group chat and answer the question.','Dana: "The audit session moved to Room 204; the projector in our usual room is broken." — Lee: "I will email everyone before the break."','NONE','["The usual room","The projector store","The break lounge","Room 204"]','D',1,'The session relocated to Room 204 because the usual projector is broken.',19),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','LAUNDRY — Machines take coins only and stop accepting cycles at 10:00 p.m. Remove your load promptly; items left after an hour may be set on the folding table.','NONE','["No cycle may be started after 10:00 p.m.","The machines accept cards","Detergent is supplied free","Left items are thrown away"]','A',1,'The sign says machines stop accepting cycles at 10:00 p.m.',20),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the alert and answer the question.','BEACH ALERT — Swimming is closed from Thursday until the rip-current warning lifts, expected Friday morning. The walking path stays open the whole time.','NONE','["Only Thursday afternoon","The whole walking path","From Thursday until about Friday morning","The entire week"]','C',1,'Swimming is barred from Thursday until the warning is expected to lift on Friday morning.',21),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the ticket and answer the question.','ADMIT ONE — Valid only on the date printed and only at the gate shown. An unused ticket can be changed once, up to 24 hours before the event.','NONE','["It may be used the day after","A change is possible up to 24 hours before","It works at any gate","It never expires"]','B',1,'The ticket may be changed once provided the request comes at least 24 hours ahead.',22),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the email and answer the question.','COURSE EVALUATION — The survey closes Sunday at 11:59 p.m. and takes about four minutes. Responses are anonymous, so we cannot reply to individual comments by email.','NONE','["The survey needs your ID number","The survey takes an hour","It stays open all month","A student cannot get a personal reply"]','D',1,'Because replies are anonymous, the office cannot send a personal response.',23),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','CAFETERIA — Coffee is free with any meal receipt until 10:30 a.m. After that, cards are accepted at the machines in both halls.','NONE','["Before 10:30 a.m. a meal receipt gives coffee at no charge","Coffee costs extra all day","Cards fail after 10:30 a.m.","The machines sit in one hall only"]','A',1,'The card states coffee is free with a meal receipt until 10:30 a.m.',24),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','RETURN SLOT — The outside bin is locked during snow removal and reopens by 8:00 a.m. Late fees count from the due date whether or not the bin was open.','NONE','["The bin never closes","A book left in the closed bin may still incur fees","Fees pause when the bin is locked","Snow days extend every due date"]','B',1,'The notice warns that fees run from the due date even if the bin was locked, so a book left inside may still accrue a fine.',25),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the schedule and answer the question.','WELCOME WEEK — Registration cards are collected on Monday, not at the Tuesday talks. Bring the card to every session to be counted.','NONE','["Tuesday","Wednesday","Monday","Friday"]','C',1,'Cards are collected on Monday, explicitly not on Tuesday.',26),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the permit card and answer the question.','RESIDENT PARKING — Permits are issued only to households, one per address, and list the plate of each car. A permit is worthless if the plate does not match.','NONE','["A permit without a matching plate is invalid","Any visitor may borrow the permit","Each car gets its own permit","A permit covers a whole street"]','A',1,'The card is void unless the car''s plate matches the listed one.',27),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the flyer and answer the question.','WRITING CENTER — Open noon to 6:00 p.m. on weekdays. The evening appointment block ends at 5:30 p.m.; walk-ins can come until 6:00.','NONE','["The center closes at noon","Walk-ins are not allowed","It opens at weekends","An appointment must be taken by 5:30 p.m."]','D',1,'Appointments run only to 5:30 p.m.; the 5:30–6:00 window is for walk-ins.',28),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the notice and answer the question.','CAMPUS SHUTTLE — Runs every 10 minutes from 7:00 a.m., dropping to every 20 minutes after 8:00 p.m. No shuttle runs between midnight and 5:00 a.m.','NONE','["Buses always come every 10 minutes","The wait can be 20 minutes late in the evening","The service never stops","A bus runs at 2:00 a.m."]','B',1,'After 8:00 p.m. the interval widens to 20 minutes.',29),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the instructions and answer the question.','FINAL EXAM — Seat numbers are posted outside the hall. Bring a photo ID; without one you will be seated at the back and your score is held until the office checks your record.','NONE','["No photo ID is required","Early arrival guarantees a seat","A student without ID sits at the back and the score is held","The office rechecks every score"]','C',1,'Without a photo ID a student is seated at the back and the score is held.',30),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the closure note and answer the question.','OFFICE CLOSURE — The bursar''s window is closed Thursday for the holiday and reopens Friday at 9:00 a.m. Payments made online are still processed Wednesday evening.','NONE','["Thursday","Friday morning","Wednesday evening","The whole holiday weekend"]','A',1,'The window is closed on Thursday for the holiday.',31),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the sign and answer the question.','LOT E — Only vehicles with three or more people may use the front rows before 9:30 a.m. Two-person carpools park in the back rows, which fill quickly.','NONE','["Three-person cars park in the back only","Rows are open to all cars","The lot shuts at 9:30 a.m.","A two-person car must park in the back"]','D',1,'Only three-plus vehicles reach the front rows, so a two-person car goes to the back.',32),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the request form and answer the question.','TRANSCRIPT — Paper grades are mailed only if you check the box on the request form and pay the copying fee; a blank box leaves the grades online only.','NONE','["Grades are mailed automatically","Paper grades need a checked box and a fee","The request carries no cost","Grades never appear online"]','B',1,'A mailed copy requires both the checked box and the fee.',33),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the dorm notice and answer the question.','QUIET HOURS — From 11:00 p.m. to 7:00 a.m. sound must not carry through the walls. A first complaint is a warning; a second can end the housing contract.','NONE','["A second complaint may end the contract","The quiet period lasts one hour","A first complaint brings a fine","Loud noise is allowed after 7:00 p.m."]','A',1,'The notice ties a second complaint to ending the contract.',34),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_DAILY_LIFE','Read the library email and answer the question.','AUTO RENEWAL — Eligible items renew by themselves once on the due date. If a second renewal is needed or another borrower has requested the item, it must be returned or brought to the desk.','NONE','["Every item renews without limit","The item must be returned each time","A requested or twice-renewed item will not renew on its own","The desk cannot help with renewals"]','C',1,'A requested item or one already renewed twice loses automatic renewal.',35),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly explain?','Every year countless animals travel distances that dwarf their own bodies: sea turtles cross entire oceans, monarch butterflies reach mountains they have never seen, and songbirds return to the same pond after wintering far to the south. For decades the leading explanation held that such travellers steer by cues they learn, following coastlines or remembering the position of stars. Evidence gathered in the last twenty years points to something subtler. Certain species appear to sense the Earth''s magnetic field, an invisible map whose strength and tilt change predictably from pole to equator. When researchers placed migratory birds in chambers where the field could be altered, the birds shifted their chosen direction to match the new setting, even with no view of the sky. Magnetic sensing likely does not act alone; smell, sunlight, and inherited programs probably combine to guide the journey.','NONE','["How animal size limits migration distance","How a magnetic sense may help migrating animals, alongside other cues","Why monarch butterflies reach the same pond","Why researchers prefer laboratory chambers"]','B',1,'The passage centres on a possible magnetic sense guiding migrants, noting it works with smell, sunlight, and inherited programs.',36),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "steer" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Every year countless animals travel distances that dwarf their own bodies: sea turtles cross entire oceans, monarch butterflies reach mountains they have never seen, and songbirds return to the same pond after wintering far to the south. For decades the leading explanation held that such travellers steer by cues they learn, following coastlines or remembering the position of stars. Evidence gathered in the last twenty years points to something subtler. Certain species appear to sense the Earth''s magnetic field, an invisible map whose strength and tilt change predictably from pole to equator. When researchers placed migratory birds in chambers where the field could be altered, the birds shifted their chosen direction to match the new setting, even with no view of the sky. Magnetic sensing likely does not act alone; smell, sunlight, and inherited programs probably combine to guide the journey.','NONE','["find food","rest","guide their course","avoid enemies"]','C',1,'Travellers use cues to steer, that is, to guide their course, so option C is closest.',37),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about the birds kept in the altered-field chambers?','Every year countless animals travel distances that dwarf their own bodies: sea turtles cross entire oceans, monarch butterflies reach mountains they have never seen, and songbirds return to the same pond after wintering far to the south. For decades the leading explanation held that such travellers steer by cues they learn, following coastlines or remembering the position of stars. Evidence gathered in the last twenty years points to something subtler. Certain species appear to sense the Earth''s magnetic field, an invisible map whose strength and tilt change predictably from pole to equator. When researchers placed migratory birds in chambers where the field could be altered, the birds shifted their chosen direction to match the new setting, even with no view of the sky. Magnetic sensing likely does not act alone; smell, sunlight, and inherited programs probably combine to guide the journey.','NONE','["They could set a direction without seeing the sky.","They refused to move at all.","They needed the coastline to orient.","Their inherited programs stopped working."]','A',1,'The birds matched their direction to the new field even with no view of the sky, so sky cues were not required.',38),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, why were the earliest tablets mostly lists?','Long before coins or paper, the record-keepers of southern Mesopotamia tracked grain with small clay tablets pressed by a cut reed. The earliest surviving examples, from roughly five thousand years ago, are lists rather than stories: a pictograph of a stalk, a notch for quantity, and a sign naming the person who received the ration. Because barley and emmer were handed out daily to workers, priests, and soldiers, an accurate count mattered more than eloquence, and the writing stayed terse and numerical. Over centuries the pictographs wore down into abstract wedges, and scribes learned a growing sign-list in temple schools. A tablet recording a beer allowance for a brewer therefore sits at the root of both bookkeeping and literature, a reminder that writing may have been invented first to remember what was owed, not what was felt.','NONE','["Because story-telling was forbidden","Because clay could not hold pictures","Because only kings could read them","Because keeping an exact count of rations mattered more than eloquence"]','D',1,'The passage says daily rations made an accurate count more important than eloquence, so the tablets stayed terse and numerical.',39),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "terse" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','Long before coins or paper, the record-keepers of southern Mesopotamia tracked grain with small clay tablets pressed by a cut reed. The earliest surviving examples, from roughly five thousand years ago, are lists rather than stories: a pictograph of a stalk, a notch for quantity, and a sign naming the person who received the ration. Because barley and emmer were handed out daily to workers, priests, and soldiers, an accurate count mattered more than eloquence, and the writing stayed terse and numerical. Over centuries the pictographs wore down into abstract wedges, and scribes learned a growing sign-list in temple schools. A tablet recording a beer allowance for a brewer therefore sits at the root of both bookkeeping and literature, a reminder that writing may have been invented first to remember what was owed, not what was felt.','NONE','["brief","decorative","confused","permanent"]','A',1,'The writing kept to short numerical entries, so "terse" = brief, which is option A.',40),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about later scribes?','Long before coins or paper, the record-keepers of southern Mesopotamia tracked grain with small clay tablets pressed by a cut reed. The earliest surviving examples, from roughly five thousand years ago, are lists rather than stories: a pictograph of a stalk, a notch for quantity, and a sign naming the person who received the ration. Because barley and emmer were handed out daily to workers, priests, and soldiers, an accurate count mattered more than eloquence, and the writing stayed terse and numerical. Over centuries the pictographs wore down into abstract wedges, and scribes learned a growing sign-list in temple schools. A tablet recording a beer allowance for a brewer therefore sits at the root of both bookkeeping and literature, a reminder that writing may have been invented first to remember what was owed, not what was felt.','NONE','["They no longer recorded grain.","They needed formal training as the sign-list grew.","They wrote only stories.","They abandoned clay tablets."]','B',1,'Pictographs became abstract wedges and the sign-list grew, learned in temple schools, implying formal training.',41),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What is the main idea of the passage?','If enough members of a community cannot be infected, a germ that enters the population may fail to find a next host and its chain of transmission breaks. Public-health researchers call this herd immunity, and the share of people who must be protected to bring it about is the threshold. The threshold is not a fixed number: a highly contagious measles requires roughly ninety-five percent of a population to be immune, whereas polio needs far fewer. Vaccination raises the protected share without exposing people to the disease itself, so reaching the threshold shields those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborns or the severely ill. The weak point is uneven coverage. A community that vaccinates uniformly can hold the line, but a cluster that declines together leaves pockets of susceptibility through which the germ travels, and the protection promised to everyone thins out.','NONE','["Measles is the most dangerous disease","Newborns should be vaccinated at once","Herd immunity depends on reaching a threshold that varies by disease","Vaccines expose people to the illness"]','C',1,'The passage defines herd immunity and a threshold that differs across diseases, then warns about uneven coverage.',42),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The phrase "thins out" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','If enough members of a community cannot be infected, a germ that enters the population may fail to find a next host and its chain of transmission breaks. Public-health researchers call this herd immunity, and the share of people who must be protected to bring it about is the threshold. The threshold is not a fixed number: a highly contagious measles requires roughly ninety-five percent of a population to be immune, whereas polio needs far fewer. Vaccination raises the protected share without exposing people to the disease itself, so reaching the threshold shields those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborns or the severely ill. The weak point is uneven coverage. A community that vaccinates uniformly can hold the line, but a cluster that declines together leaves pockets of susceptibility through which the germ travels, and the protection promised to everyone thins out.','NONE','["spreads widely","doubles","moves out of sight","becomes weaker"]','D',1,'When unvaccinated pockets let the germ travel, community-wide protection weakens, so "thins out" = becomes weaker.',43),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, why are clusters that decline vaccination together risky?','If enough members of a community cannot be infected, a germ that enters the population may fail to find a next host and its chain of transmission breaks. Public-health researchers call this herd immunity, and the share of people who must be protected to bring it about is the threshold. The threshold is not a fixed number: a highly contagious measles requires roughly ninety-five percent of a population to be immune, whereas polio needs far fewer. Vaccination raises the protected share without exposing people to the disease itself, so reaching the threshold shields those who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborns or the severely ill. The weak point is uneven coverage. A community that vaccinates uniformly can hold the line, but a cluster that declines together leaves pockets of susceptibility through which the germ travels, and the protection promised to everyone thins out.','NONE','["They raise the herd threshold for measles","They leave openings through which the germ can travel","They protect newborns directly","They make the germ more contagious"]','B',1,'A cluster that declines together leaves pockets of susceptibility through which the germ travels.',44),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','According to the passage, how does a signed language refer back to a person?','A common mistake is to treat signed languages as finger spelling or as loose gestures that pantomime speech. Linguists who study them report something very different. American Sign Language, Japanese Sign Language, and dozens of others have the equipment of any spoken tongue: a stock of basic units, rules for combining them, and ways to mark tense, question, and negation. A signer uses the space in front of the body to place a person or a field and then points back to it, an economy that hearing grammar achieves with pronouns. Word order bends to carry emphasis, and small changes in hand shape flip a meaning. That signed grammar is full and rule-governed matters outside the lecture hall; it shapes how children born deaf acquire language and how schools should teach them.','NONE','["By placing the person in space and pointing back to that spot","By spelling the name with fingers only","By copying the spoken word exactly","By changing the size of the sign"]','A',1,'The signer locates a person in space and then points back, the equivalent of a pronoun.',45),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The word "pantomime" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','A common mistake is to treat signed languages as finger spelling or as loose gestures that pantomime speech. Linguists who study them report something very different. American Sign Language, Japanese Sign Language, and dozens of others have the equipment of any spoken tongue: a stock of basic units, rules for combining them, and ways to mark tense, question, and negation. A signer uses the space in front of the body to place a person or a field and then points back to it, an economy that hearing grammar achieves with pronouns. Word order bends to carry emphasis, and small changes in hand shape flip a meaning. That signed grammar is full and rule-governed matters outside the lecture hall; it shapes how children born deaf acquire language and how schools should teach them.','NONE','["translate silently","avoid","act out by gestures","argue against"]','C',1,'The mistaken view treats signs as gestures that act out speech, so "pantomime" = act out by gestures.',46),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage suggest about schools that treat signing as loose gesture?','A common mistake is to treat signed languages as finger spelling or as loose gestures that pantomime speech. Linguists who study them report something very different. American Sign Language, Japanese Sign Language, and dozens of others have the equipment of any spoken tongue: a stock of basic units, rules for combining them, and ways to mark tense, question, and negation. A signer uses the space in front of the body to place a person or a field and then points back to it, an economy that hearing grammar achieves with pronouns. Word order bends to carry emphasis, and small changes in hand shape flip a meaning. That signed grammar is full and rule-governed matters outside the lecture hall; it shapes how children born deaf acquire language and how schools should teach them.','NONE','["They follow the best research","They teach deaf children faster","They rely too much on finger spelling","They misunderstand the language and its teaching needs"]','D',1,'Because signed grammar is full and rule-governed, a school that calls it loose gesture misjudges how deaf children acquire language.',47),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What does the passage mainly argue?','An astronomer needs a dark sky, and darkness has become scarce. Street lamps, shop signs, and office towers pour light upward, and that glow scatters in the atmosphere until the night that once showed the Milky Way fades to a pale grey. The trend threatens more than wonder: professional observatories depend on faint light from distant objects, and stray glow from a town many kilometres away can wash out a long exposure. In response some communities fit shields that aim lamps downward, exchange bright white bulbs for warmer ones, and dim street lights after midnight. A handful of regions have passed lighting laws, and a few mountain sites remain famous chiefly because no town grew near them. Dark sky, on this account, is not empty space but a resource that must be managed.','NONE','["That street lamps should be banned outright","That artificial light degrades the night sky and observatory work, and can be managed","That mountain sites are too remote to use","That the Milky Way will vanish this decade"]','B',1,'The passage links upward artificial light to lost darkness and weakened observations, then lists shields, warmer bulbs, and laws as remedies.',48),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','The phrase "wash out" in the passage is closest in meaning to:','An astronomer needs a dark sky, and darkness has become scarce. Street lamps, shop signs, and office towers pour light upward, and that glow scatters in the atmosphere until the night that once showed the Milky Way fades to a pale grey. The trend threatens more than wonder: professional observatories depend on faint light from distant objects, and stray glow from a town many kilometres away can wash out a long exposure. In response some communities fit shields that aim lamps downward, exchange bright white bulbs for warmer ones, and dim street lights after midnight. A handful of regions have passed lighting laws, and a few mountain sites remain famous chiefly because no town grew near them. Dark sky, on this account, is not empty space but a resource that must be managed.','NONE','["dim or spoil","clean off","carry away","double in size"]','A',1,'Stray glow ruins a long exposure by reducing its detail, so "wash out" = dim or spoil.',49),
(@set05_exam_id,'READING','READ_PASSAGE','What can be inferred about the few mountain sites still famous for dark skies?','An astronomer needs a dark sky, and darkness has become scarce. Street lamps, shop signs, and office towers pour light upward, and that glow scatters in the atmosphere until the night that once showed the Milky Way fades to a pale grey. The trend threatens more than wonder: professional observatories depend on faint light from distant objects, and stray glow from a town many kilometres away can wash out a long exposure. In response some communities fit shields that aim lamps downward, exchange bright white bulbs for warmer ones, and dim street lights after midnight. A handful of regions have passed lighting laws, and a few mountain sites remain famous chiefly because no town grew near them. Dark sky, on this account, is not empty space but a resource that must be managed.','NONE','["They have replaced every lamp with shields","They pass the strictest lighting laws","They now host most observatories","They stayed dark mainly because no nearby town developed"]','D',1,'The passage says these sites remain famous chiefly because no town grew near them.',50);
